.TH DATAWALL 1 "2026-11-17" "datawall 1.3.0" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
datawall \- track daily network usage per program
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B datawall
.RI [ command ]
.RI [ argument ]
.br
.B datawall
.B \-\-version
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B datawall
runs a lightweight background daemon that samples the kernel's network
counters every five seconds and attributes each delta to the processes that
caused it, using exact per-socket byte counters read through
.BR ss (8)
(SOCK_DIAG). It reports per-program daily totals in a terminal table, with
current transfer speeds and an optional daily traffic limit.
.PP
With no command, the daily report is shown.
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
.B report
Show today's usage per program. This is the default.
.TP
.B start
Start the background daemon, detached from the terminal.
.TP
.B run
Run the daemon in the foreground. Used by the systemd unit.
.TP
.B stop
Stop the background daemon.
.TP
.B restart
Stop and start the daemon.
.TP
.B status
Report daemon health: pid, how long ago it last sampled, the monitored
interface, current speed, today's usage against the limit, and whether the
systemd user unit is active. See
.B EXIT STATUS
below.
.TP
.B reset
Discard today's accumulated data.
.TP
.B watch
Real-time per-process bandwidth monitor, in the alternate screen. The table
is capped to the terminal height; the remaining processes are folded into a
single summary row. Press
.B Ctrl+C
to exit.
.TP
.BI days " N"
Aggregated report for the last
.I N
days, with a daily average.
.TP
.B limit
Show the daily limit, how much of it has been used, and whether desktop
notifications are active.
.TP
.BI limit " SIZE"
Set the daily traffic limit.
.I SIZE
accepts a plain byte count or a suffix:
.BR 5GB ,
.BR 500MB ,
.BR 100kb .
.TP
.B limit off
Remove the daily limit.
.TP
.B connections
Snapshot of active TCP connections, grouped by program and remote address.
.TP
.BR explain " [" \fISECONDS\fR ]
Diagnose where
.B unattributed
traffic comes from. Samples for
.I SECONDS
(4 by default), then reports how much of the interface traffic could be
attributed to TCP sockets, the TCP/UDP protocol mix, and the likely causes
ranked by severity. Sample for longer while something is downloading to get
a useful reading.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-\-version
Print the version and exit.
.TP
.B \-h, \-\-help
Print a usage summary and exit.
.SH "UNATTRIBUTED TRAFFIC"
The
.B unattributed
row is not a program. It is the difference between what the interface
counted and what the visible TCP sockets could explain. The usual causes,
by typical impact, are a VPN tunnel below the monitored interface; UDP
traffic, which
.B ss \-tpin
cannot see and which carries QUIC/HTTP3 video; protocol header overhead,
since socket counters report payload only; sockets owned by other users or
by root, whose owner the kernel hides from an unprivileged process; and
sockets that open and close between two samples.
.B datawall explain
measures which of these applies.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I ~/.datawall/store.json
Durable history: per-day totals and per-program breakdown. Written only when
the accumulated bytes change.
.TP
.I ~/.datawall/live.json
Volatile daemon state: current speed and monitored interface. Rewritten every
sampling cycle and read by
.B report
and
.BR status .
.TP
.I ~/.datawall/config.json
Configuration. See
.B CONFIGURATION
below.
.TP
.I ~/.datawall/daemon.pid
Pid of the running daemon.
.TP
.I ~/.datawall/daemon.log
Daemon log.
.SH CONFIGURATION
.I ~/.datawall/config.json
is a JSON object. All keys are optional.
.TP
.B daily_limit
Daily traffic limit in bytes. Set it with
.BR "datawall limit" .
.TP
.B interface
Pin the monitored interface by name, bypassing auto-detection. By default the
interface holding the IPv4 default route is used, and loopback and virtual
interfaces are never selected.
.TP
.B retention_days
How many days of history to keep. Default 90.
.TP
.B notifications
Set to
.B false
to disable desktop notifications.
.TP
.B notify_thresholds
Array of percentages of the daily limit that trigger a notification.
Default
.BR "[80, 100]" .
.SH "EXIT STATUS"
Most commands exit 0 on success and 1 on a usage error.
.B datawall status
is more specific:
.TP
.B 0
The daemon is running and sampling.
.TP
.B 1
The daemon is stopped.
.TP
.B 2
The daemon's process is alive but it has stopped sampling.
.SH EXAMPLES
.TP
Start the daemon and watch traffic live:
.EX
datawall start
datawall watch
.EE
.TP
Cap usage at 5 GB a day and be notified:
.EX
datawall limit 5GB
.EE
.TP
Find out why so much traffic is unattributed:
.EX
datawall explain 30
.EE
.TP
Last week's usage:
.EX
datawall days 7
.EE
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR ss (8),
.BR ip-route (8),
.BR systemctl (1),
.BR notify-send (1)
.SH AUTHOR
Erysnell Gonzalez
.SH BUGS
Report issues at
.UR https://github.com/Erysnell/datawall
.UE .
